it's been about a week since Apple  surprised us with these new MacBook Pros  and I wanted to give you guys an update  on my experience upgrading from my  15-inch I almost forgot which one was -  the new 16 inch now this video isn't a  review which we are working on and taken  our time with to make sure that it's  filled with great detailed info that  many aren't talking about but in this  video I'll be sharing the real-world  differences that I noticed as I've been  using this new 16 inch MacBook Pro both  the good things and the bad first off  let's talk about the keyboard I know a  lot of people hate it on the butterfly  keys but I wasn't one of them  upgrading to these new keys did feel  better right off the bat but it wasn't a  huge deal to me personally but I do have  to say that I've been pretty lucky with  my butterfly keyboards not having any  issues that was until I got used to  these new keys and then switched back  wow these are way better and they are  not just the old pre butterfly keys but  from what I could tell they are the best  of both worlds with much larger key  travel and the reliability of the pre  2016 keys fused with the better  stability and more mechanical feel of  the butterfly keys overall these are a  major win I'm someone that really uses  my MacBook Pros mics so apart from that  first little comparison we did during  hands-on I haven't really needed to use  the so-called studio microphones that  are included with the MacBook Pro  because we have a lot of high quality  microphones but I have watched a few  videos on MacBook Pros where youtubers  would switch from the regular mics to  the MacBook Pro and then the MacBook Pro  would actually sound a lot better so I  do think that for a lot of people  they're actually gonna be able to use  these microphones in an upgrade from  what they already have or just not to  buy anything extra now let's talk about  a slight disappointment the speaker's  don't get me wrong they sound fantastic  the bass is much improved in the  separation of frequencies due to the  extra speakers is great but my  disappointment is volume  [Music]  [Music]  it's not bad still beating out most if  not all Windows laptops of similar size  and those that are actually bigger and  heavier but I did have several occasions  where I wish that they were louder  just like the 15-inch MacBook or that  came previously which are about three  decibels louder especially if you're  playing back in loud environments or if  you have a bunch of group of people say  and you want to show a trailer or  something like that it is nice to be  able to just crank it up now let's talk  about that 16 inch display to be honest  when I was hearing rumors about this new  MacBook I was hoping that with this  redesign we would get something like  what Dell offers with their infinity  edge displays but nope the bezels did  shrink a bit but it's not a big enough  difference to just jump out at you now  on the other hand if you have a 15-inch  MacBook Pro the larger 16 inches slate  definitely does jump out at you at first  and then when I went back to the 15-inch  that's when I really noticed the  difference in screen size and the bezels  with that said nobody should be  upgrading for the larger screen or the  better speakers alone now what you  should upgrade for is the graphics with  the release of the eight core MacBook  Pros there was a big boost in CPU  performance but the graphics stayed the  same meaning for tasks that really push  the GPU the CPU was actually  bottlenecked so how have the new 7  nanometre AMD Navi graphics been well  it's a mixed bag if you already have a  bag of 20 as I do the 550 M is  definitely much more powerful if you're  pushing your graphics to the extreme  limits say in video editing especially  in Bruce X where I saw a huge difference  or even bigger and blender where the  four gigs of video memory you've really  hammer it and bottled like the graphics  but in my regular use real world even  including some video editing using 4k  h.264 files and final cuts I didn't see  that much of a difference it really  matters what you are doing with your  computer and how heavy the graphics card  is being hit with that said if you have  a Mac without Vega graphics you will  definitely notice a difference even in  easier video editing now I will be doing  more in depth detail  testing with video editing across a  couple different editing programs and  codecs but that will be on my max yuryev  channel so go ahead and check that out  now we're graphics really come in is  gaming the Vega 20 MacBook was the first  portable Mac that could play triple-a  titles some way I decently and this  year's upgrade is a huge one the 5500 M  is about 50 percent faster than the Vega  20 which is a huge improvement just one  year later after the Vega but what is  even more impressive is the base 5300 M  which I also gamed with and that is  almost as powerful as the best option in  smokes the Vega 20 while costing 450  dollars less I know a lot of you guys  were asking about fortnight's so I did  play some in Mac OS and the experience  was great I was getting over 60 FPS at  1440p with mostly high settings if you  drop the resolution to just under 1080p  for perfect scaling you can play with  the settings maxed out super smooth no  issues at all  and that was actually on the base 16  inch macbook pro we also played the  latest call of duty with the 5500  amusing boot camp and had a great  experience staying above that 60 FPS  goal with medium settings out 1440p  resolution now the base i7 5300  performed basically the same but we are  thinking that is because these are brand  new drivers boot camp was just made  available to drivers and I know  performance is going to get better now  this isn't a gaming powerhouse so you  shouldn't buy it for gaming but what is  very important is that these base  graphics are now about three times as  fast as just a week ago and can actually  compete with Windows laptops like the  Dell XPS 15 which has a 1650 now Apple  really emphasizes the upgraded cooling  in this MacBook Pro and yes it does make  a difference in performance comparisons  but do you actually notice it in the  real world no not really the performance  difference is definitely there but  unless you're actually using a timer or  running benchmarks you're not really  gonna notice it so I say it is not  enough to warrant upgrading from the  same chips in a 15-inch chassis but one  thing that I did notice that was nicer  is that the fans are less annoying the  fans themselves have a deeper sound but  it's  about a decibel louder at full blast and  rendering videos I did notice the fans  more so but during less intensive tasks  and even doing some video editing the  fans run slower so overall it is  definitely an improvement now I wish I  can say that the battery life is also  better but at this time I really can't  tell I did do some kind of simple more  casual tests and actually seemed like  the sixteen-inch was draining more  battery but I don't want to say that  until I have some concrete evidence on  some more detail testing so I'll make  sure that is included in the full review  overall I am really impressed with the  new 16 inch MacBook Pro it is absolutely  a better machine all around but what is  even more important is that the value  proposition is massively better  especially with updated apps that use  metal you could honestly get similar if  not better performance this year on a  2400 our machine not this one I hear but  one that I've over there the base model  compared to last fall's MacBook Pro  which costs over $1,000 more just like  mine right here actually which does hurt  a bit but at the same time I am very  happy the Apple is making these changes  go ahead and click above on that circle  if you want to subscribe to see our full  detailed review which you do not want to  miss and click over there to see another  great MacBook Pro video this is an  actual smacks deck and I will see you in  the next one  [Music]

